--TEST--
A `Core\Heap<T>` interleaving pushes with pops still answers each `pop` with the element that sorts first among everything it then holds, and a sweep that never pushes below its last `pop` drains in non-decreasing order — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 9, ADR 0013
--FILE--
<?mwl
// § 9's heap is a priority queue, so its promise is not "the table sorted" —
// a push after a pop may arrive below everything already there, and the next
// pop is allowed to answer below the previous one. The invariant that does
// hold at every step is local: whatever `pop` answers, nothing left behind
// sorts before it. `heap-orders-by-comparable-or-by-its-comparator.mwlt`
// reads a drained order off its own line; this asks the question of every
// element still in the heap after every pop and **counts** the answers, so a
// sift that goes wrong one position deep fails here rather than printing a
// plausible-looking sequence.
final class Order {
    // A positive entry pushes; `0` pops, guarded, so a pop against the empty
    // heap in the middle of the table is a step and not the throw
    // `heap-holds-every-push-and-refuses-an-empty-read.mwlt` pins. Returns the
    // number of (popped, still held) pairs found in order — a violation
    // lowers it, and so does a sweep that quietly stopped popping.
    public static function pairsInOrder(Core\Heap<int> $h, array<int> $ops, bool $ascending): int {
        int $ordered = 0;
        foreach ($ops as int $op) {
            if ($op == 0) {
                if (!$h->isEmpty()) {
                    int $out = $h->pop();
                    // ADR 0053 § 3's cursor is a read, so the heap is intact
                    // for the next step of the table.
                    foreach ($h as int $left) {
                        if ($ascending) {
                            if ($left >= $out) {
                                $ordered = $ordered + 1;
                            }
                        } else {
                            if ($left <= $out) {
                                $ordered = $ordered + 1;
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            } else {
                $h->push($op);
            }
        }
        return $ordered;
    }

    // The monotone half: how many adjacent pops came out in non-decreasing
    // order. A table whose pushes never go below the last pop makes this the
    // whole drained sequence.
    public static function risingPairs(Core\Heap<int> $h, array<int> $ops): int {
        int $rising = 0;
        int $previous = 0;
        bool $started = false;
        foreach ($ops as int $op) {
            if ($op == 0) {
                if (!$h->isEmpty()) {
                    int $out = $h->pop();
                    if ($started) {
                        if ($out >= $previous) {
                            $rising = $rising + 1;
                        }
                    }
                    $previous = $out;
                    $started = true;
                }
            } else {
                $h->push($op);
            }
        }
        return $rising;
    }
}

// Eight pops, one of them against the empty heap and so skipped, leaving
// 2+2+1+2+2+1+0+0 = 10 pairs to ask about. The pushes of 1 and of 4 both
// arrive below the element popped just before them, which is exactly the
// interleaving a globally sorted reading would get wrong.
array<int> $ops = [7, 2, 9, 0, 4, 0, 0, 1, 8, 0, 3, 0, 0, 0, 0, 5, 0];

var $rising = new Core\Heap<int>();
var $falling = new Core\Heap<int>(fn (int $a, int $b): int => $b - $a);
echo "in order ", Order::pairsInOrder($rising, $ops, true), " ", Order::pairsInOrder($falling, $ops, false), " of 10\n";
echo "spent ", $rising->count(), $falling->count(), "\n";

// Monotone: 3 and 1 go in, 1 comes out, and every later push (2, 7, 5) is at
// or above the last value popped. The drained sequence is then 1 2 3 5 7 —
// non-decreasing across all four of its adjacent pairs, which is the promise
// a scheduler actually leans on.
array<int> $monotone = [3, 1, 0, 2, 7, 0, 5, 0, 0, 0];
echo "non-decreasing ", Order::risingPairs(new Core\Heap<int>(), $monotone), " of 4\n";
--EXPECT--
in order 10 10 of 10
spent 00
non-decreasing 4 of 4
